  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nThe aforementioned upper level ridge will slowly shift east and \nbuild over the Mississippi Valley this weekend, while an upper \ntrough moves from the Southwest to the Four Corners area. With \nmoistening low-level south-southeast flow, expect an increase in \ncloud cover during the weekend. Temperatures will be above normal, \nand the increase in low-level moisture and cloud cover will reduce \nto some extent the daily diurnal range in temperatures. Expect highs \nto be mostly in the upper 70s to lower 80s Saturday and mid 70s to \naround 80 on Sunday. Surface pressure gradient and nocturnal low-\nlevel jet development will support somewhat gusty south to southeast \nwinds over the weekend.   \n\nThe upper trough will move east across Colorado/New Mexico Sunday \nnight and into the central/southern Plains on Monday. Expect showers \nassociated with this trough to occur in western and northwestern \nparts of Texas Sunday, but a few showers may develop or move into \nour far western counties in the afternoon and evening. With some \nupper support with the aforementioned trough, the area generally \nwest of a Haskell to San Angelo to Sonora line will have a low \nchance (15-30 percent) of showers and possibly a thunderstorm Monday \nafternoon. Isolated to scattered showers and a few storms will be \npossible across a larger/expanded part of our area Monday night and \nTuesday. A weak cold front may move as far south as our northern \ncounties by Tuesday. This would provide an additional focus for \npossible showers and a few storms. Daily highs should trend down \ninto the mid to upper 70s Monday, and lower to mid 70s on Tuesday. \nWednesday looks dry and a little warmer when a shortwave upper ridge \nis progged to move over Texas.",
